---
// Figure: a primary without persistence that restarts at once wipes its replica.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './repl.css';
interface Props { number: number; failoverFigure: number }
const { number, failoverFigure } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="rp-wipe" title="A fast restart wipes the replica" controls="steps"
  caption={`A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. Both versions were run on Redis 8.10.3 on my laptop with three Sentinels at default settings and two replicas; the figure draws one. In the first, both replicas were empty within 10 seconds of the crash; the second is the failover of Figure ${failoverFigure}.`}>
  <Choice slot="params" name="restart" label="After the crash, the primary" value="restart" options={[{ value: 'restart', label: 'restarts at once' }, { value: 'down', label: 'stays down' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>A primary without persistence, Redis A, and its replica, Redis B, each hold 5,000 keys, watched by Sentinel. The primary crashes.</p>
    <p>If it is restarted at once, it is back in under a second with no data and a new replication ID. Sentinel’s pings are answered again long before its 30-second limit, so it does nothing. The replica reconnects, cannot continue from where it was, receives a full copy of the empty dataset, and drops its 5,000 keys. Both servers end up empty, within 10 seconds of the crash in my run.</p>
    <p>If the primary stays down, Sentinel marks it down after 30 seconds and promotes the replica with its 5,000 keys. When the old primary returns, empty, it becomes a replica and copies them.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { wipeSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="rp-wipe"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, wipeSpecs, 'restart'));
</script>
